WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:10.019
Atlas License Directory. A monthly printed directory that is checked, frozen, printed, verified and published - and can be rolled back.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:20.697
Scene 1. Four separate files arrive, as they would from four different systems. One example of every problem the rules know about has been planted. Six. The

00:00:20.697 --> 00:00:30.345
missing venue token and the near-duplicate name are advisory: reported, not blocking. The repeated row is simply collapsed.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:40.046
Scene 2. Every file is fingerprinted and counted, values are cleaned, and the rulebook runs. Each blocking finding names the record and the reason. No. Six

00:00:40.046 --> 00:00:48.242
blocking findings stop the run at the check stage. The QA summary is written instead.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:58.110
Scene 3. The editor fixes the sources. This time the checks pass - but freezing needs a named person's approval, and nobody has given it. The approval gate.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:06.437
Clean data is necessary but not sufficient; a person must sign off before printing.

00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:16.452
Scene 4. With approval the snapshot is frozen and twelve PDFs are printed into staging. Then the process is stopped on purpose, before anything is checked or

00:01:16.452 --> 00:01:24.988
published. No. Publishing only happens after verification, and the files are still in staging.

00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:35.914
Scene 5. Resume reads the checkpoints. Stages whose output still has the same fingerprint are skipped; the remaining ones run, each PDF is verified, and the set

00:01:35.914 --> 00:01:45.444
is published in one step. Only verify and publish. Check, freeze and render are proven done by their hashes.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:55.893
Scene 6. December's data arrives with three new listings and two withdrawn. It goes through the same line and is published, with a report of what changed.

00:01:55.893 --> 00:02:04.935
Nothing. It stays in published/2026-11, untouched; only the pointer moves to December.

00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:15.394
Scene 7. A problem is reported with December. Rollback re-checks every November file against its manifest and points readers back at November. No. It is kept;

00:02:15.394 --> 00:02:24.404
only the pointer changes. And if any November file had been altered, rollback would refuse.

00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:31.947
The contents page: every entry is a link, and every page number was planned before drawing.

00:02:33.717 --> 00:02:38.153
A craft section: each listing printed from the frozen snapshot.

00:02:39.983 --> 00:02:45.098
The QA summary: every finding with its rule, records and fix.

00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:51.061
December compared with November: what was added and withdrawn.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:03:00.157
What this run showed: Six bad records blocked, each one named. Nothing printed before a named sign-off. A crash mid-print resumed without redoing work. Every

00:03:00.157 --> 00:03:07.464
PDF verified; rollback re-hashes first.
